WEBVTT

cue-VID_U1_02-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Every product travels through sectors. Bramblefield Farms gets raw milk from its cows. Meadowline Dairy turns that milk into cheese. Harbourmart Grocers sells the cheese to shoppers. The primary sector extracts natural resources. The secondary sector manufactures goods from those raw materials. The tertiary sector provides services to consumers and other firms. Follow the flow lines: raw milk, processed cheese, then a retail service at the shop.

cue-VID_U1_02-2
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:52.000
The primary sector extracts and uses the natural resources of Earth to produce raw materials used by other businesses. Bramblefield Farms operates here. Its cows grazing on farmland supply essential raw milk, and the farm sells that milk to a processor. Because the primary sector supplies food and raw materials, other sectors depend on it. Farming, mining and forestry all belong to this sector.

cue-VID_U1_02-3
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:18.000
The secondary sector manufactures and processes goods using raw materials from the primary sector. Meadowline Dairy receives Bramblefield's raw milk and pasteurises it, then makes cheese and yoghurt. Those are physical, tangible goods that can be touched. Construction and car building are also secondary activity. Because processing adds a usable product, the dairy can sell to retailers for more than the raw milk cost.

cue-VID_U1_02-4
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:44.000
The tertiary sector provides services to consumers and to other sectors of industry. Harbourmart Grocers retails the cheese made by Meadowline Dairy. Retailing is a service: the shopper gets convenience, not a physical product made there. Banking, insurance, transport and teaching are tertiary too. Services are non-physical and intangible; they cannot be held. Because shoppers want easy access, the retailer creates value by placing goods near them.

cue-VID_U1_02-5
00:01:44.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Follow one kilogram of cheese through the chain. Bramblefield Farms supplies raw milk worth two dollars. Meadowline Dairy pays that, processes the milk and sells the cheese to Harbourmart Grocers for three dollars twenty. The dairy has added one dollar twenty. Because the grocer then sells to a shopper for four dollars fifty, it adds one dollar thirty more. Therefore the same product passes through primary, secondary and tertiary stages, and value grows at each step.

cue-VID_U1_02-6
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Economies change over time. Industrialisation is the growth of manufacturing, as factories and secondary-sector jobs expand. Meadowline Dairy's grandparent once worked in a large local factory. De-industrialisation occurs when there is a decline in the importance of the secondary, manufacturing sector in a country. Factory jobs fall and service jobs grow. Because incomes rise and demand shifts, therefore employment moves toward tertiary work.

cue-VID_U1_02-7
00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:10.000
A mixed economy contains both private and public sectors. The private sector is owned by private individuals and companies aiming for profit. Harbourmart Grocers competes on price and service, which can improve efficiency. However, profit-seeking firms may avoid essential services that are not profitable. The public sector is owned and controlled by government and often provides key services. Therefore a mixed economy balances market choice with essential provision, but taxes must fund it.

cue-VID_U1_02-8
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:40.000
Time for an application check. Bramblefield Farms milks cows, Meadowline Dairy bottles and pasteurises the milk, and Harbourmart Grocers sells it. Which sector is each firm in? The farm is primary because it extracts a natural resource. The dairy is secondary because it processes that resource into a good. The grocer is tertiary because it sells a service to shoppers. Watch each block slide into its sector as the flow line lights up.

